---
/**
 * Typography for the legal documents.
 *
 * Written as explicit descendant rules rather than pulled in with `@tailwindcss/typography`.
 * The plugin is excellent and would be the right call for a blog; here it is a dependency
 * and a stylesheet's worth of defaults to override, for two pages whose entire content is
 * headings, paragraphs, lists and one table. This is smaller than the overrides would be.
 *
 * Legal text is read in two ways that ordinary page copy is not: skimmed for one clause, and
 * printed. So headings are given room to be scannable, and `global.css` has a print block.
 */
---

<div
  class="prose-legal max-w-none text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-content/90 [&_a]:text-primary [&_a]:underline [&_a:hover]:text-content [&_blockquote]:border-l-2 [&_blockquote]:border-primary/40 [&_blockquote]:pl-4 [&_blockquote]:text-muted [&_code]:rounded [&_code]:bg-raised [&_code]:px-1.5 [&_code]:py-0.5 [&_code]:text-[0.9em] [&_h2]:mt-12 [&_h2]:mb-4 [&_h2]:text-2xl [&_h2]:font-bold [&_h2]:text-content [&_h3]:mt-8 [&_h3]:mb-3 [&_h3]:text-lg [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:text-content [&_hr]:my-10 [&_hr]:border-line [&_li]:my-1.5 [&_ol]:my-4 [&_ol]:list-decimal [&_ol]:pl-6 [&_p]:my-4 [&_strong]:font-semibold [&_strong]:text-content [&_table]:my-6 [&_table]:w-full [&_table]:text-left [&_td]:border-t [&_td]:border-line [&_td]:py-2 [&_td]:pr-4 [&_th]:pb-2 [&_th]:pr-4 [&_th]:font-semibold [&_ul]:my-4 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-6"
>
  <slot />
</div>
